<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # How Are You? – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> I'm Happy.</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> How Are You?</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Express likes or dislikes using simple words and pointing.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can express likes or dislikes about feelings and preferences using simple phrases and gestures (e.g., pointing and saying, 'I like being calm.' 'I don't like feeling tired.'). Can respond to questions about feelings and needs with simple 'I'm...' statements (e.g., 'How are you?' 'I'm excited.' 'I'm tired.'). Can use 'I'm...' phrases along with gestures to express emotions and basic needs (e.g., saying 'I'm hot.' while fanning themselves or 'I'm sad.' with a frown).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; text-align: left;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; text-align: left;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; text-align: left;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Feelings Song and Action" activity. Students sing along to a simple song about feelings (e.g., "If You're Happy and You Know It") and perform actions for different emotions like 'happy', 'sad', 'angry', 'sleepy', 'excited', 'tired'. The teacher uses large feeling flashcards (happy, sad, tired, hungry, thirsty, excited, calm, angry, sleepy, sick, warm) and prompts students to show the feeling with their faces and bodies.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the question "How are you?" and responses like "I'm happy!" with exaggerated gestures and facial expressions, pointing to the corresponding flashcards. The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of /h/ in 'happy' and /e/ in 'excited' by saying the words slowly and clearly, having students repeat chorally. The teacher also points out the letter 'H' in 'happy' and 'E' in 'excited' on the flashcards, reinforcing phonemic awareness.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a 'happy' flashcard) "Is this boy crying or smiling?" (Smiling)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a 'hungry' flashcard) "When you are hungry, do you want to play or eat?" (Eat)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "I'm Happy."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple, illustrated short story or dialogue featuring two friends asking "How are you?" and responding with various feelings (e.g., "I'm happy," "I'm tired," "I'm hungry"). The story shows what they "need" or "want" related to their feelings (e.g., "I'm sleepy, I need to rest." "I'm hungry, I want to eat."). The teacher uses large, colorful picture cards to present the story visually.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Expressing feelings and needs by saying, 'I'm happy.' or 'I'm tired.'; Responding to questions about feelings by answering 'How are you?' with simple 'I'm...' statements (e.g., 'I'm excited.').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions:</b> "Who is happy in the story?" (Point to character). "Is the girl tired or excited?" (Point to correct feeling picture).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Matching:</b> Teacher shows two large picture cards: one of a child saying "I'm hungry" and another showing food. Students point to the matching food for "hungry". Repeat for "sleepy" and "rest", "sad" and "hug".</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice saying "I'm [feeling adjective]" and asking/answering "How are you?". They will use vocabulary words: happy, sad, tired, hungry, thirsty, calm, sleepy, excited, sick, warm, angry; verbs: smile, frown, hug, help; nouns: friend. Grammar focus: 'I'm + adjective' and 'How are you?' WH-question.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Flashcard Response):</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard depicting a feeling (e.g., 'happy'). Students, chorally and individually, say "I'm happy!" The teacher repeats with all target feeling flashcards (happy, sad, tired, hungry, thirsty, calm, sleepy, excited, sick, warm, angry), prompting students to use appropriate gestures (e.g., 'frown' for sad, 'smile' for happy, fanning for 'warm'). Emphasize /h/ and /e/ sounds.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice (Teacher-Student Q&A):</b> The teacher asks individual students, "How are you?" Students look at a displayed feelings chart or choose a flashcard and respond, "I'm [feeling word]." The teacher encourages a variety of responses and prompts with gestures. (e.g., Teacher: "How are you, [Student's Name]?" Student: (points to 'thirsty' picture) "I'm thirsty!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Feelings Exchange):</b> Students stand face-to-face with a 'friend'. The teacher models: "Ask your friend 'How are you?' and answer 'I'm [feeling].'" Students take turns asking and answering. The teacher circulates, providing support and correction, encouraging them to use gestures (e.g., 'I'm sleepy' with closed eyes, 'I'm excited' with a jump). Introduce simple 'I like...' or 'I don't like...' statements, e.g., "I like being calm." "I don't like feeling angry."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "I'm Happy."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Feelings Role-Play Station." Students will work in pairs or small groups (3 students) at designated stations. Each station has a basket with various feeling flashcards, small toys or props representing needs (e.g., a blanket for 'sleepy', a toy food for 'hungry', a small ball for 'play', a small book for 'rest').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One student (Student A) picks a flashcard and expresses the feeling using "I'm..." and gestures (e.g., picks 'sick' card, says "I'm sick," and pretends to cough).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Another student (Student B) asks, "How are you?" and then offers something related to the feeling, e.g., "Do you need help?" or "Do you want to rest?" (using the props).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A responds, expressing a preference or need: "Yes, I need to rest," or "No, I want to play." They can also say, "I like being calm" or "I don't like feeling tired."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students rotate roles so everyone gets a chance to express feelings and respond. The teacher moves between groups, facilitating and encouraging natural interaction, focusing on fluency and communication, and ensuring correct use of "I'm..." statements and the question "How are you?". Introduce letters W, X, Y, Z by finding them on feeling words or other words in the environment if possible (e.g., 'W' for 'want', 'X' for an 'eXcited' character name, 'Y' for 'you', 'Z' for 'dizzy' if discussing 'sick').</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Feelings Role-Play Station." The checklist focuses on participation, use of target language, and clarity of communication.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student will be observed as they: (1) ask "How are you?" to a peer, (2) respond with an "I'm [feeling]" statement, (3) use a gesture corresponding to the feeling, and (4) express a simple like or dislike (e.g., "I like being happy").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Clear pronunciation of /h/ in 'happy' and /e/ in 'excited' and other feeling words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Uses at least three different feeling words (e.g., happy, sad, tired).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of "I'm..." statements:</b> Clearly states "I'm happy," "I'm sad," etc.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of gestures:</b> Employs gestures to reinforce the expressed feeling or need.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Actively participates and interacts with peers during the task.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students receive a paper with three emotion faces (happy, neutral, sad). They circle the face that shows how they feel about talking about feelings. Below, they complete the sentence: "Today I can say: I'm _______."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new feeling words did you learn or practice today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you tell a friend 'How are you?' and answer 'I'm [feeling]'?" (Thumbs up/down)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The "Feelings Role-Play Station" worked well for encouraging interaction and using gestures. Students showed good understanding of "I'm..." statements and "How are you?". Some students still need more practice with specific vocabulary, especially 'thirsty', 'calm', and 'sick'. For next time, I need to provide more specific sentence frames for expressing likes/dislikes (e.g., "I like ______" or "I don't like ______") to increase their speaking output and explicitly review letters W, X, Y, Z with more targeted examples. I will prepare simpler visual aids for the less common feelings.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This speaking practice of expressing feelings will directly feed into Lesson 4 (Writing Composition), where students will construct simple sentences about how they feel and what they like/dislike (e.g., "I'm happy. I like to play.") using the same vocabulary and grammar structure.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>